Equivalence Proof
Claim. The spread and excess formulas for variance are equivalent. \[  
\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{ \left\{ * \right\}{ Y - \mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y) }^2 } =\mathop{\mathrm{E}}[ Y^2] - \left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y)}^2
\]
\[
\small{
\begin{aligned}
\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{ \left\{ * \right\}{ Y - \mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y) }^2 }
&= \mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{ Y^2 - 2 Y \mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y) + \left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y)}^2 } && \text{ FOIL } \\
&= \mathop{\mathrm{E}}[ Y^2] - \mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{2 Y \mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y)} + \mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{\left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y)}^2} && \text{Distributing expectations (linearity)} \\
&= \mathop{\mathrm{E}}[ Y^2] - 2\mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y)\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{Y} + \left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y)}^2\mathop{\mathrm{E}}[1] && \text{Pulling constants out of expectations (linearity)} \\
&= \mathop{\mathrm{E}}[ Y^2] - 2\left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y)}^2 + \left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y)}^2] && \text{Recognizing a product of something with itself as a square} \\
&= \mathop{\mathrm{E}}[ Y^2] - \cancel{2}\left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y)}^2 + \cancel{\left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y)}^2}] && \text{Subtracting}
\end{aligned}
}
\]

Conditional Variance as Excess
\[
\small{
\begin{aligned}
\mathop{\mathrm{\mathop{\mathrm{V}}}}(Y \mid W) &= \mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{ \left\{ * \right\}{ Y - \mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y\mid W)}^2 \mid X } && \text{ the \emph{spread} idea: the mean square of a conditionally centered version of $Y$ } \\
&= \mathop{\mathrm{E}}\p*{Y^2 \mid W} - \left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\p*{Y \mid W}}^2              && \text{ the \emph{excess} idea: the average amount $Y^2$ exceeds the square of its cond. mean}
\end{aligned}
}
\]










We can think of the Conditional Variance as excess, too.
It’s the same as the unconditional case, but within subpopulations.

Proof
Claim. \[
\mathop{\mathrm{\mathop{\mathrm{V}}}}[Y] = \textcolor{blue}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{\mathop{\mathrm{\mathop{\mathrm{V}}}}(Y \mid W)}} + \textcolor{green}{\mathop{\mathrm{\mathop{\mathrm{V}}}}\sb*{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y \mid W)}}
\]

Rewrite the variances, conditional and unconditional, in excess form.


\[
\mathop{\mathrm{\mathop{\mathrm{V}}}}[Y]  = \textcolor{blue}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{ \mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y^2 \mid W) - \left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\p*{Y \mid W}}^2}}
                       + \textcolor{green}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\left\{ * \right\}{ \mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y \mid W)^2}  - \p*{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\p*{Y \mid W}}}^2}
\]


Distribute the outer expectations in the first term.


\[
\mathop{\mathrm{\mathop{\mathrm{V}}}}[Y] = \textcolor{blue}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{ \mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y^2 \mid W)} - \mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{\left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\p*{Y \mid W}}^2}}
                       + \textcolor{green}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{ \left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y \mid W)^2} } - \p*{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\p*{Y \mid W}}}^2}
\]


Cancel matching terms


\[
\mathop{\mathrm{\mathop{\mathrm{V}}}}[Y]  = \textcolor{blue}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{ \mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y^2 \mid W)} \cancel{- \mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{\left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\p*{Y \mid W}}^2}}}  
                       + \textcolor{green}{\cancel{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{ \left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y \mid W)^2} }} -  \p*{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\p*{Y \mid W}}}^2}
\]


Use the law of iterated expectations to simplify the remaining terms.


\[
\mathop{\mathrm{\mathop{\mathrm{V}}}}[Y]  = \textcolor{blue}{E[Y^2]  \cancel{- \mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{\left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\p*{Y \mid W}}^2}}}
                       + \textcolor{green}{\cancel{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\sb*{ \left\{ * \right\}{\mathop{\mathrm{E}}(Y \mid W)^2} }} - (\mathop{\mathrm{E}}\left\{ * \right\}{Y})^2}
\]

