The covariate distributions \(p_{x \mid 0}\) and \(p_{x \mid 1}\) are very simple. \(p_{x \mid 1}\) is flat. At each of the 5 education levels, you have 1/5 of the female residents. \(p_{x \mid 0}\) is linear. At the level \(x=14\), you have 1/5 of the male residents. But the fraction decreases by \(1/20=.05\) with every two years of education, \[\color{gray}
\begin{aligned}
\textcolor[RGB]{0,191,196}{p_{x \mid 1}} &= \frac{1}{5} \qqtext{ and }
\textcolor[RGB]{248,118,109}{p_{x \mid 0}} &= \frac{1}{5} - \frac{1}{20} \frac{x-14}{2}.
\end{aligned}
\] More importantly, they have a linear relationship, \[
\color{gray}
\begin{aligned}
\textcolor[RGB]{248,118,109}{p_{x \mid 0}} &= \textcolor[RGB]{0,191,196}{p_{x \mid 1}} \times (ax + b) \qfor a=\frac{11}{4} \qand b=-1/8.
\end{aligned}
\]

